cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Embedded Linux Top 20: the audited kit cookbook"
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Pagaran
    given-names: Joseph Ambrose
    orcid: ""
abstract: >-
  Twenty bench labs on one fixed parts bin and four Linux hosts, where every
  pairing was checked against headers, voltages and silicon before it was
  written down. Six rules decide what a lab may do: one 40-pin expansion board
  per host, a 24-pin host that accepts none of them, 3.3 V logic throughout, one
  instrument per lab, and a Linux host that owns storage, time, the network and
  the interface while a microcontroller only feeds it. The labs cover a Cat-4
  default route with event compression, an NB-IoT power-saving duty cycle, a
  Cat-M node that timestamps from satellite rather than from the host clock, a
  wideband vibration and ultrasound gateway over a serial gadget, a calibrated
  analogue recorder at 100 kS/s, multi-zone ranging painted on a small display,
  two sensor shields kept deliberately apart, a non-Raspberry host with its own
  storage-wear question, a source-measure energy bench, a real-time clock and
  device-tree console, an operator display with a local broker, signalling
  failover across two radios, a serial provisioning jig, a mixed-voltage safety
  lab, two wireless sidecars, a high-rate serial recorder, an offline tilt
  instrument, and a four-host fleet sweep. Each lab carries the correction that
  an earlier architectural draft of the same material needed, printed beside the
  lab rather than hidden in a changelog, and an acceptance test written to be
  failable.
keywords:
  - embedded linux
  - raspberry pi
  - device tree
  - sensor integration
  - cellular IoT
  - data acquisition
  - energy measurement
  - bench method
license: MIT
